    model: 'The inferior alveolar nerve block targets the inferior alveolar nerve, a branch of the mandibular division (V3) of the trigeminal nerve, which supplies the mandibular teeth. Anesthetic is deposited on the medial surface of the ramus near the mandibular foramen, where the nerve enters the mandible. Within the mandible the nerve continues forward and exits through the mental foramen as the mental nerve, which supplies the lower lip and chin, so they become numb along with the teeth. The lingual nerve runs close by and is usually anesthetized at the same time, numbing the side of the tongue. Numbness lasting days rather than hours suggests paresthesia, possibly from nerve trauma, which should be documented and followed. The maxillary molars are instead supplied by the posterior superior alveolar nerve, a branch of the maxillary division (V2), and are usually anesthetized by infiltration or a PSA block.',
