    model: 'Tooth #19 in the Universal system is the mandibular left first molar; in the FDI system it is 36, and in Palmer notation it is written as a 6 in the lower-left bracket (┌6). Tooth #3 is the maxillary right first molar, FDI 16, and Palmer 6 in the upper-right bracket (6┘). MOD means the restoration covers the mesial surface (the proximal surface toward the midline), the occlusal surface (the chewing surface) and the distal surface (the proximal surface away from the midline). The maxillary first molar’s occlusal surface has four major cusps (mesiobuccal, distobuccal, mesiolingual and distolingual), sometimes a cusp of Carabelli, an oblique ridge from the mesiolingual to the distobuccal cusp, mesial and distal marginal ridges, and fossae and pits where developmental grooves meet, especially the central fossa. These deep pits, fissures and fossae trap plaque and are hard to clean with a toothbrush, so they are the most common sites of Class I caries; sealants and good hygiene help prevent them.',
