  frq: {
    prompt: 'A 62-year-old is missing her mandibular right first molar (#30). Compare three ways to replace it (a fixed bridge, a removable partial denture and an implant), naming the components of each and explaining the advantages, disadvantages and steps or care for each option.',
    rubric: [['Describes fixed bridge with abutments (#29 and #31), retainers, pontic, connectors; pros/cons (fixed, preparing adjacent teeth)', ['abutment', 'pontic', 'retainer', 'prepar', 'fixed']], ['Describes removable partial denture with clasps, rests, framework and base; pros/cons (lower cost, removable, less stable, care)', ['partial denture', 'clasp', 'framework', 'base', 'removable']], ['Describes implant with fixture, abutment, crown and osseointegration; pros/cons (preserves bone, no tooth prep, surgery, time, cost, peri-implantitis)', ['implant', 'fixture', 'abutment', 'osseointegration', 'bone']], ['Discusses patient factors and care (bone volume, health, smoking, hygiene, cost, maintenance)', ['bone', 'smok', 'diabetes', 'hygiene', 'cost', 'clean']]],
    model: 'A fixed bridge would use the second premolar (#29) and second molar (#31) as abutments; each is prepared for a crown that acts as a retainer, and the retainers are connected to a pontic that replaces #30, forming a three-unit bridge. It is fixed, stable and relatively quick, but it requires removing healthy tooth structure from the abutments and cleaning under the pontic with floss threaders. A removable partial denture would use a metal framework with clasps and rests on remaining teeth and an acrylic base holding a replacement tooth. It costs less and requires little tooth preparation, but it is less stable, can feel bulky, must be removed and cleaned daily and puts stress on clasped teeth. An implant would place a titanium fixture in the bone at the #30 site; after several months of osseointegration, an abutment is attached and a crown placed. It preserves bone, does not affect neighboring teeth and functions much like a natural tooth, but it requires surgery, adequate bone (possibly grafting), more time and cost, and careful hygiene to prevent peri-implantitis. Her bone volume, medical conditions such as diabetes, smoking, hygiene habits and budget would guide the choice.',
  },
  sources: [['American Association of Oral and Maxillofacial Surgeons: Procedures', 'https://myoms.org/what-we-do/'], ['American College of Prosthodontists: Patient Resources', 'https://www.gotoapro.org/'], ['ADA MouthHealthy: Dental Implants', 'https://www.mouthhealthy.org/all-topics-a-z/dental-implants'], ['ADA MouthHealthy: Whitening', 'https://www.mouthhealthy.org/all-topics-a-z/whitening'], ['StatPearls: Alveolar Osteitis', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK582144/']],
